### Did Kenosha police have knowledge or reasonable suspicion at the time that Kyle Rittenhouse was the person who had fired the shots before he left the area?
Answer: Based on the search result, Kenosha police were being explicitly told by bystanders that Rittenhouse was the shooter before he left the area. As Rittenhouse approached the police tactical vehicles on Sheridan Road, "Someone yells, 'Hey, he just shot them! Hey, dude right here just shot them!,' repeating that several times," and when asked "Him?" the person "responds affirmatively, 'Him.'" Despite these warnings, Rittenhouse was not stopped or detained by police—he waved at one police vehicle "but it continues onward," and "A second police vehicle also passes him." The article describes him raising his hands as he approached the police, even attempting to approach a squad car, but no officers stopped him. This suggests police had at least some level of knowledge (via bystanders' explicit warnings) that he was involved in the shootings, yet he was allowed to leave the area and was not taken into custody until the following day in Illinois.

